.TH WAVE 1 "" "MacWave" "MacWave Manual"

.SH NAME
wave \- a package manager for macOS software developers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wave
\fIcommand\fR [\fIpackage\fR] [\fIflags\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
MacWave installs each package into its own versioned directory under the
install root, keeps every version side by side, and maintains
unversioned links so a package can be called with or without a version.
Packages are fetched from a mirror or from MacWave's own bottle store;
nothing is compiled on the user's machine.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install
Install a package, or one specific version of it. Several packages can
be given at once, separated by commas; each may carry its own @version.
.TP
.B upgrade
Upgrade a package to its latest version; with --all, every installed
package. Old versions are kept — -X, --remove-old-versions removes them
in the same run.
.TP
.B reinstall
Download and install the same version again, replacing what is on disk.
Useful when a package was rebuilt under the same version number (4.6
relocated Mach-O files to @loader_path), when files were damaged, or to
pin a package back to the version already installed instead of
upgrading.
.TP
.B uninstall
Uninstall a package version. Several packages can be given at once,
separated by commas; a package may carry several @versions, or @all for
every installed version. The unversioned link falls back to the highest
remaining version, and goes away with the last one.
.TP
.B list
List the packages you have installed, with their versions, in two
columns.
.TP
.B search
Search the online package index.
.TP
.B info
Show author, description, homepage and the installed / available
versions.
.TP
.B selfupdate
Update MacWave itself to the version published in the self-update
repository.
.TP
.B link
Create a versionless shortcut, or re-point it at another installed
version. Installs and uninstalls keep it up to date afterwards.
.TP
.B unlink
Remove the versionless shortcut. A later uninstall will not re-create
it.
.TP
.B linkquery
Show which version the versionless shortcut points at, as name =>
version. With no package (or with --all) it lists every installed
package, two columns when the terminal is wide enough.
.TP
.B mirror
Show the address each kind of data is fetched from, including the
built-in defaults for anything not overridden by an environment
variable.
.TP
.B latest
Compare what you have with the newest version in the index; nothing is
downloaded.
.TP
.B basedir
Print MacWave's install root -- the base_dir in config.json, which is
where the binaries, the links and the dependencies live. A path under
your home directory is shown with ~. With --raw it prints the absolute
path as-is and nothing else. With --set it copies the whole tree to a
new location and rewrites base_dir; that needs three typed
confirmations, and every installed artifact must already be
position-independent (run wave transfer --all first).
.TP
.B transfer
Rewrite the Mach-O references of already installed packages so they no
longer name an absolute install path, then ad-hoc sign them again.
Packages that are already position-independent are detected and skipped,
so the command is safe to repeat. Dependencies used by the named
packages are transferred too, otherwise the tree still could not be
moved.
.TP
.B shellenv
Print the shell code that prepends MacWave's directories to PATH. You
eval it once from your shell profile; it does nothing on its own.
.TP
.B version
Print the installed MacWave version.
.TP
.B manual
Show the full manual — the same text that ships as man pages and info
files — or just one command's section.

.SH GLOBAL FLAGS
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-silent
Print nothing at all; the exit code is the signal (beats -v; install /
upgrade / uninstall / link / unlink / selfupdate)
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-show\-error
With -s, still print the error when a command fails (without the 🌊, so
scripts can parse it)
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-continue
Resume an interrupted download
.TP
.B \-\-proxy <string>
HTTP/HTTPS proxy (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8080)
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-ssl
Skip SSL certificate verification (insecure)
.TP
.B \-\-limit\-rate <string>
Limit download speed (e.g. 200K, 1M, 5M; a bare number means bytes)
.TP
.B \-\-max\-time <string>
Maximum total time for one request (40s, 1min, 1min20s, 2h; a bare
number means seconds)
.TP
.B \-\-connect\-timeout <string>
Maximum time for the handshake
.TP
.B \-\-content\-time <string>
Maximum time to receive data
.TP
.B \-\-ver <string>
Install a specific version (install only; --ver=1.0 works too)
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-force
Install anyway when the running macOS is older than the package declares
.TP
.B \-\-install\-python\-from\-source
Fetch the bundled Python from its upstream URL instead of MacWave's own
repository
.TP
.B MACWAVE_PIP_INDEX
Env var. pip index for the Python deps; default pypi.org/simple
.TP
.B MACWAVE_CACERT
Env var. PEM file with extra root certificates for corporate TLS
inspection or a private-CA mirror (unset = system trust store)

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
wave install jq
wave upgrade jq
wave reinstall jq
wave uninstall jq
wave list
wave search json
wave info jq
wave selfupdate
wave link jq
wave unlink jq
wave linkquery jq
wave mirror
wave latest jq
wave basedir
wave transfer jq
eval "$(~/.local/macwave/lib/wave shellenv)"
wave version
wave manual
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACWAVE_API_DOMAIN
package index prefix (default: the public mirror)
.TP
.B MACWAVE_BOTTLE_DOMAIN
package payload prefix
.TP
.B MACWAVE_DEPS_DOMAIN
dependency payload prefix
.TP
.B MACWAVE_CACERT
PEM file with extra root certificates (corporate CA)
.TP
.B MACWAVE_PIP_INDEX
pip index for the Python dependencies
.TP
.B MACWAVE_TEST
treat this directory as the filesystem root (for tests)
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/macwave_config/config.json
user-level install location
.TP
.B /opt/macwave_config/config.json
system-level install location (takes priority)
.TP
.B <base_dir>/man/man1
these man pages
.TP
.B <base_dir>/info
the info manual (wave.info, wave-zh.info)
.SH SEE ALSO
wave-manual(1), info(1), wave(1)\&
